#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133694 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "234800a9-ae9c-56c4-8284-84041f129a59" :question/math-academy-id "e-8759" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy^2+ 2xz.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $\\mathbf r(t)= \\langle e^{t}, t, e^{-t}\\rangle$ for $t\\in \\left[0,1\\right],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨e,1,\\frac{1}{e}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,1)$ and $\\left(e, 1, \\dfrac{1}{e}\\right).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(e,1,\\frac{1}{e})-f(1,0,1) \\\\  & =(e(1)^{2}+2\\cdot e\\cdot \\frac{1}{e})-(1(0)^{2}+2(1)(1)) \\\\  & =(e+2)-2 \\\\  & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133695 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "667923e0-14bc-5bbd-8129-333703cd6616" :question/math-academy-id "e-8760" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t,  1-t \\right\\rangle, \\quad 0 \\leq t \\leq 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle{\\int _C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\textrm d\\mathbf r}$ for $\\mathbf F = ye^{xy}\\,\\mathbf i + xe^{xy}\\,\\mathbf j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $\\mathbf F = \\nabla f.$ Since we're given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= e^{xy}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f = \\mathbf F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,0)-f(0,1) \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot 0}-{e}^{0\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =1-1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133706 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "3f724b05-571d-529d-ab2b-db9dd6d2b531" :question/math-academy-id "e-9502" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)= xe^{y}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x + 2y=3$ for $x\\in [-1,1],$ evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_{C} \\nabla f\\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $\\mathbf r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $\\nabla f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_{C} \\nabla f \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf r = f(\\mathbf r(b)) - f(\\mathbf r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle t, \\dfrac{1}{2}(3-t)\\right\\rangle, \\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot {e}^{1}-(-1)\\cdot {e}^{2} \\\\  & =e+{e}^{2} \\\\  & =e(1+e).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "324fc058-3716-52e2-915c-a8ff8548ce66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238492} {:answer/id #uuid "40f10544-0cd1-50c6-9897-c035b59965c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238616} {:answer/id #uuid "99009bfa-183e-50ad-b31d-4a8cd8b1b4fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239384} {:answer/id #uuid "ae9e232f-a132-5ae1-a985-ee43b1e859ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-{e}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239575} {:answer/id #uuid "b4578f1a-d647-5132-b9c6-b097afd1fc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4578f1a-d647-5132-b9c6-b097afd1fc07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239632} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc00398-8a1c-5911-a2b1-71601fb149ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45e3e133-a99c-58ca-b74f-4cbfe235bb72" :question/math-academy-id "q-104625" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y={x}^{3}+1$ for $x\\in [1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{3}+1⟩,\\,t\\in [1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,9⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,2)$ and $(2,9).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(1)) \\\\  & =f(2,9)-f(1,2) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-{e}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\cdot 1-{e}^{1}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cb86dc4-a7cd-5ca1-b598-954b892dcccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238168} {:answer/id #uuid "23431fc6-f04f-5360-af6d-30876ddc94bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "146" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238365} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcde37b-9a32-506b-bf91-9dcabb76716e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "198" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239270} {:answer/id #uuid "9b44e22a-7576-5afa-b3b8-4a77db65eb5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239409} {:answer/id #uuid "a90ce1ba-c1cf-56f7-b6bb-6a5a1b7fdca0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cb86dc4-a7cd-5ca1-b598-954b892dcccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "171" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238168} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae2dfe3-e747-5345-997b-435580cc53ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "893763e7-56bb-5bf7-9f2b-99426982714b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104626" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}).$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ for $x\\in [-1,2],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,2{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t\\in [-1,2].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨-1,2⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2,8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(-1,2)$ and $(2,8).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2))-f(r(-1)) \\\\  & =f(2,8)-f(-1,2) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({2}^{3}+{8}^{3})-\\frac{1}{3}((-1)^{3}+{2}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(520)-\\frac{1}{3}(7) \\\\  & =\\frac{520}{3}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =171.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415107 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6f935a-7227-56de-9d74-d58f3261b90a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415071} {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} {:answer/id #uuid "75de655b-bac7-5de2-9e4c-ce56729e4a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415080} {:answer/id #uuid "87f317ce-1ebd-53fe-bd1c-2786b6ebd37d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415081} {:answer/id #uuid "db14b874-47ec-5b46-a18d-4f53face6c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "376dde01-d46a-5414-9e84-a7d099ab2f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415075} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fdf3bea-7332-5701-aa59-1894b6d9cc82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3d23b94-95d1-5af0-9020-b1e5d5ab8286" :question/math-academy-id "q-104637" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2}{e}^{xy}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0)$ and $(0,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f((r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(0,2)-f(1,0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (0)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{0(2)}-0\\cdot (1)^{2}\\cdot {e}^{1(0)} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ceeb53b-391b-5fef-9ab5-1097ef938825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415073} {:answer/id #uuid "2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415074} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b875c4-e194-5a11-823c-e0a27f17aa6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415086} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5489e8-7985-5f2a-b47e-2c5f4e8a4b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415089} {:answer/id #uuid "d782c18b-724f-503c-9bd1-3a2cfd6e6827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f083f8e-a1a4-56c3-aea6-e98468b6122b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415074} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b783c0-b546-54f4-ac0c-0b114a515648" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a59364c1-7975-54ce-83e7-b32f8ae82486" :question/math-academy-id "q-104639" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}{z}^{3}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{4},{t}^{6}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,1).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,1)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{3})-(0\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {0}^{3}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e699747-9682-50ad-bc3c-89217394ac15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238310} {:answer/id #uuid "2607fd46-ddb8-5e68-96ad-e64aed38f812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238385} {:answer/id #uuid "897eb260-f9ce-5892-bb70-7962baeb2fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239250} {:answer/id #uuid "95fcd466-856f-5f24-95dd-68c6e3fb2527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239353} {:answer/id #uuid "e5429a9a-ce6f-502f-af4d-a0421c2ce2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95fcd466-856f-5f24-95dd-68c6e3fb2527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239353} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7cb5bc-2de6-517b-a738-225b68f34e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "377033ea-e89e-5b49-a8b3-206ce2c51bb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104640" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}z+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(2\\pi ) & =⟨1,0,2\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(1,0,0)$ and $(1,0,2\\pi ).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(2\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,0,2\\pi )-f(1,0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 2\\pi +1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)-({1}^{2}\\cdot 0+1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =(2\\pi +0)-0 \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240920 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fb1fd90-967a-508f-964a-ddcc8ad1fec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238329} {:answer/id #uuid "38d3c833-662c-50cc-95fd-d02f93ab6c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238545} {:answer/id #uuid "780f23b5-b5bf-596a-b047-13dbffe326e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239094} {:answer/id #uuid "c5aaa345-119e-5e65-bc94-2528387dcd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239795} {:answer/id #uuid "de389670-0e34-52aa-9dc2-f2ea5b75eb5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239998}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5aaa345-119e-5e65-bc94-2528387dcd96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239795} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8418831-a814-5787-ba6f-30266ff0bc9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e735c8c7-7a76-5786-82b2-e7e846ae7fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-104731" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2t,{t}^{2}-1⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2xy-y)\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2xy-y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,-1⟩, \\\\ r(2) & =⟨4,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,-1)$ and $(4,3).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(4,3)-f(0,-1) \\\\  & =({4}^{2}\\cdot 3-4\\cdot 3)-({0}^{2}\\cdot (-1)-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =48-12 \\\\  & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415105 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415072} {:answer/id #uuid "62d13908-df4e-538d-9943-7c76652cbac9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415079} {:answer/id #uuid "aad335b1-b4de-568f-b2e2-0f2fa5a05048" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415084} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a24286-6aba-57bd-af78-4a37875e9372" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415085} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ede2a6-76f1-5387-b6b8-e8fb84166eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29a72800-4e23-513e-92c2-5437578df2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415072} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9c0c78-b891-5f32-8dca-852634763b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9399f137-a4f5-51b7-a84e-790d740d6a70" :question/math-academy-id "q-104732" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t,\\frac{\\pi t}{2}⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y)\\,i-({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-x)\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+xy.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{1}{2}) & =⟨1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{4}))-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+0\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}+\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab61eea-ba71-5224-bbe8-eca2df0fe157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238278} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0e41b7-21e7-573e-b5d2-f356608a6ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi -1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238703} {:answer/id #uuid "ace99781-1604-53b3-b08e-f26c241a358f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239557} {:answer/id #uuid "c2892dd7-dcc3-567c-8bcb-ac89416a6e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239762} {:answer/id #uuid "e7de7023-d488-540d-962c-5b17cc9d176b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ab61eea-ba71-5224-bbe8-eca2df0fe157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238278} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ef2ea0-0a2c-50ba-bcc4-0d5182b81264" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86f7c787-e5df-5794-8bba-c906aa91522f" :question/math-academy-id "q-104739" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y={x}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡x$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi ].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{e}^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2}+{sin}^{2}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi ,{\\pi }^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-({0}^{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(0+{e}^{{\\pi }^{2}})-(0+1) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\pi }^{2}}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b3b744-39b6-57dd-83b1-ca255a0e5591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238414} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7d66b3-3253-501c-9d23-d0a00aa17af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239429} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a48cb6-3488-5f91-a510-56e98f26b7eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{ln}^{2}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239614} {:answer/id #uuid "d222d7af-bcc2-54b0-bd8f-b14855923fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{ln}^{2}⁡2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239900} {:answer/id #uuid "f773a812-93fa-544d-b4f9-b7fcdc706cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7d66b3-3253-501c-9d23-d0a00aa17af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186239429} :answer-field/id #uuid "61dfdacb-ab42-5b45-8340-88f93ef0c7b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186240512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "13fd31c7-58b2-51a7-95e7-235c3524f10b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104761" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2x{e}^{-y}\\,i+(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for $x\\in [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)={{2x{e}^{-y}}_{⏟}}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+{{(2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y})}_{⏟}}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-2x{e}^{-y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{-y}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y-{x}^{2}{e}^{-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{-y}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-1)⟩,\\,t\\in [2,3].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨2,0⟩, \\\\ r(3) & =⟨3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(2,0)$ and $(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(3,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-f(2,0) \\\\  & =({3}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)^{2})-({2}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{-0}+{0}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2-4 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+{ln}^{2}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "302b8581-4a68-5c5c-8795-9744e9d4a2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257206} {:answer/id #uuid "953e0310-1f6a-5dcb-9428-9966108f8f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258094} {:answer/id #uuid "b72e2f4d-4041-5680-a701-550b3147bc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "87" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258378} {:answer/id #uuid "ca82cadd-75e5-598b-bacc-17ec432f7824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258541} {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5b95c82-3200-5da8-b666-432aa9c1aa8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "106" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258928} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b2dc29a-3749-5531-9860-c3324dac8a28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "003b8481-988f-56eb-a4c6-1362503d86f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-124002" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}+x+y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},t+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,4],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ r(4) & =⟨2,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,5).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(4))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(2,5)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =({2}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{2}+2+5)-({0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}+0+1) \\\\  & =107-1 \\\\  & =106.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259587 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133878 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b643761-8eab-5bd3-a5f5-c85b6ba34a61" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Parametric Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "543ba384-51b6-557c-bf16-43ac82ca0e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257502} {:answer/id #uuid "bfd16522-1981-538e-a666-611647c995ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258455} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6cd51-6e8f-5103-b8e1-9cf9c4743823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc320e3-b118-5e6b-bdc4-5e336a48b133" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258824} {:answer/id #uuid "f7bffceb-ddbe-55f4-ac78-cfd9d1162748" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2c6cd51-6e8f-5103-b8e1-9cf9c4743823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}+2e-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258755} :answer-field/id #uuid "63cb2a76-38cc-5ba0-833b-b7d3644b3675" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "26e83fe0-4239-5444-b048-589ba5747a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-124008" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={e}^{x}+{e}^{y}+{e}^{z}.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,{t}^{2},2{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0,0⟩ \\\\ r(1) & =⟨1,1,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0,0)$ and $(1,1,2).$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(1))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(1,1,2)-f(0,0,0) \\\\  & =({e}^{1}+{e}^{1}+{e}^{2})-({e}^{0}+{e}^{0}+{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =(2e+{e}^{2})-3 \\\\  & ={e}^{2}+2e-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0794e38d-83c6-592a-8698-603e9bbcd5de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256847} {:answer/id #uuid "239089df-2146-51b4-b115-a88f3d39c9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257080} {:answer/id #uuid "79dbb24e-2d02-5659-8575-c9dca1acb841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257858} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3ff693-01df-587b-b8f8-2302b9d2cfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258036} {:answer/id #uuid "bd179ab0-00b3-5bab-8759-a6525529b5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd179ab0-00b3-5bab-8759-a6525529b5da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258432} :answer-field/id #uuid "7766e048-d9fa-5296-ac66-21288dbdd49b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e457dc8-8930-562f-905f-f48a2ebaba17" :question/math-academy-id "q-124029" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve ${y}^{2}=x$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "572cddd3-8f84-5b08-8f30-ba3696a9b1fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi }-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257535} {:answer/id #uuid "980d5c51-5b40-5f56-bfb7-99d5b6db0c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2\\pi }-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258122} {:answer/id #uuid "ba90b885-3030-535b-a6d6-b20cfce89c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258416} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f40706-748a-5462-8a78-9770b7b5dbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258776} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fcb8ed-37e1-5237-bf6c-95c7528a9627" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba90b885-3030-535b-a6d6-b20cfce89c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258416} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdaa45ba-790a-5b06-b275-79f77436acf0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b4571c4-6fd7-5557-9855-d69404faa890" :question/math-academy-id "q-124032" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $y=2x$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\frac{t}{2},t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi )-f(0,0) \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-{e}^{0}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & ={e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133890 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4519f3b-0fb8-54fd-9e9e-fca3e599b80b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals Over Cartesian Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47b3eeba-4eac-5d44-9adc-3211c30eb7ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257409} {:answer/id #uuid "5a534628-6707-5097-afac-65143ed14073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257569} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad476ab-38fd-5e4e-8d0a-5341b7b7b7a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257574} {:answer/id #uuid "7dec0392-9c5a-5bf9-bc94-0852a51e8494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257896} {:answer/id #uuid "d412fdac-65be-56fb-9312-852052cc11df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a534628-6707-5097-afac-65143ed14073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257569} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ade967-0b40-518f-b3ab-2d00cfb750fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c2ccfca-31f7-5fd8-9901-48e158e0be78" :question/math-academy-id "q-124035" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={y}^{2}-x.$ Given that $C$ is the path along the curve $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nWe start by parametrizing the curve $C.$ A suitable parametrization is\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nThen, the position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨1,0⟩, \\\\ r(\\pi ) & =⟨-1,\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r & =f(r(\\pi ))-f(r(0)) \\\\  & =f(-1,\\pi )-f(1,0) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}-(-1))-({0}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =({\\pi }^{2}+1)+1 \\\\  & ={\\pi }^{2}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133879 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8a1d966-b4a8-5af8-8b10-063cacea6526" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Fundamental Theorem of Line Integrals When the Potential Function Is Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bae757-f490-594c-be1d-7a2d2c77f021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360982} {:answer/id #uuid "2d6c9f0e-8b69-578c-b5ae-a56b0b59e2e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361041} {:answer/id #uuid "356d8fc5-06cd-5609-b39b-96eddcad3b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361072} {:answer/id #uuid "be29d524-2692-5e36-bb76-b3ac0de7b124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361688} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0cfc9c-4409-56c9-956d-a96016bee45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea0cfc9c-4409-56c9-956d-a96016bee45d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361880} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8db0f63-bf1b-537c-b12e-a90d68712f38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff7ad628-63c0-5640-b2d2-82a36608b04e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98995" :question/problem "Consider the curve $C,$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}),t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi t}{2})⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $F=x{y}^{2}\\,i+{x}^{2}y\\,j.$ \n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f.$ Since we're given that $F$ is conservative, we know that such a function exists. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =x{y}^{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. The position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,1⟩, \\\\ r(1) & =⟨2,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}∇f\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,1)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {2}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {0}^{2}\\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =2-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
